{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>And he placed [his hands] on the head of Ephraim.</b> To count them as tribes, like ״ויסמוך את ידיו עליו״, and from this they learned to count cavalrymen in this manner." "<b>For he crossed his hands.</b> He did this with insight and wisdom, for by right he should have switched the sons according to what he intended — to place Ephraim at his right and Manasseh at his left. Instead he applied the insight and wisdom to his hands, for he did not wish to shame Manasseh, since he was the firstborn and he needed to show him some honor. Another interpretation: <b>For Manasseh was the firstborn</b> — even though Manasseh was the firstborn, he did not place his right hand on him, for he saw by divine spirit that his younger brother would become greater than him. And there are many instances of כי whose meaning is 'even though,' such as ״רפאה נפשי כי חטאתי לך״, and ״וסלחת לעוני כי רב הוא״, ״דרך ארץ פלשתים כי קרוב הוא״, and many more."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 48, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:09:04.429806408Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}